event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_06117ef87a5610ec006a629e17864881908c48718937619cce","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":320,"text":"The latest Perseverance-specific news is that NASA says the rover has found evidence that a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rocks on the rim of Jezero Crater was formed by repeated asteroid impacts. The rock sequence, nicknamed the “Broom Point member,” is likely more than 3.9 billion years old, making it among the oldest terrain ever studied by a Mars rover. NASA says this gives scientists a rare look at a very early, violent period in solar-system history that Earth’s geology has largely erased through plate tectonics 【5:3†source】.\n\nA second recent update is that Perseverance has effectively “run a marathon” on Mars: it has driven 26.2 miles / 42.195 km across the planet. It reached that milestone on sol 1,890 of its mission, much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance 【5:0†source】. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter also captured Perseverance from orbit around the time of the milestone, showing the rover and its tracks from above 【5:2†source】.\n\nNASA’s recent Perseverance news page also highlights new imagery and mapping tied to “Broom Point,” including views of layered bedrock around Jezero’s rim and the route the rover took to reach that region 【5:1†source】. Overall, the rover appears to be in an active science phase beyond Jezero Crater’s original floor, studying very old rocks that could reveal Mars’ early geologic and impact history."}

